(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["arrays",{"l:arrays/python-lists-references-capacity-and-amortised-a":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A Python \u003ccode>list\u003c/code> is a \u003cb>dynamic array of references\u003c/b>: one contiguous block of 8-byte pointers to separately allocated objects, plus a length and a larger \u003cb>capacity\u003c/b>. Indexing is address arithmetic, so O(1). Appending is \u003cb>amortised O(1)\u003c/b>: when the block is full, CPython reallocates it about one eighth larger, and the occasional O(n) move is paid for by the cheap appends before it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>PyListObject                    ob_item: one contiguous block of pointers\n+---------------+              +------+------+------+------+\n| ob_size   = 3 |              |  *   |  *   |  *   | free |   capacity 4\n| allocated = 4 |              +--|---+--|---+--|---+------+\n| ob_item   = *-+-------------&gt;     |      |      |\n+---------------+                   v      v      v\n                                 int 7  float 3.5  str \"hi\"   (separate objects)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Memory.\u003c/b> On 64-bit CPython 3.12, \u003ccode>sys.getsizeof([])\u003c/code> is 56 bytes and every slot of capacity adds 8. The elements are separate objects: an \u003ccode>int\u003c/code> is 28 bytes (ints from -5 to 256 are cached and shared), a \u003ccode>float\u003c/code> 24. A list of a million distinct floats therefore takes about 32 MB, while \u003ccode>array('d')\u003c/code> or a NumPy \u003ccode>float64\u003c/code> array stores the raw values contiguously in 8 MB, and NumPy loops over them in C.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Growth rule.\u003c/b> When an append needs more room, \u003ccode>list_resize\u003c/code> computes \u003ccode>new_allocated = (newsize + (newsize &gt;&gt; 3) + 6) &amp; ~3\u003c/code>: one eighth extra plus a constant, rounded down to a multiple of 4. Appending one item at a time gives capacities 0, 4, 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 52, 64, 76, 92, ... The list shrinks only when its size falls below half its capacity. \u003ccode>[0] * n\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list(range(n))\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>extend\u003c/code> size the block exactly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why amortised O(1).\u003c/b> With growth factor r &gt; 1, the moves at successive resizes form a geometric series whose largest term is below n, so n appends move fewer than n · r / (r - 1) references: under 2n for doubling, about 9n for CPython's 1.125. Growing by a constant k instead moves about n² / (2k): quadratic. (\u003ccode>realloc\u003c/code> may extend the block in place, so these are upper bounds.)\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>a[i] = x\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>len(a)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>address arithmetic\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>append(x)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) amortised\u003c/td>\u003ctd>work at the end of the block\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>insert(i, x)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop(i)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>del a[i]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n - i)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>every later pointer shifts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>x in a\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>a.index(x)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>a.count(x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>linear scan with \u003ccode>==\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>a[i:j]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>a.copy()\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>a + b\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(size of result)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>new block, references copied\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>C++ \u003ccode>std::vector\u003c/code> and Rust \u003ccode>Vec\u003c/code> are the same structure but store values inline, without boxing; \u003ccode>reserve(n)\u003c/code> pre-sizes them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class DynArray:\n    \"\"\"Dynamic array: size &lt;= capacity; grow geometrically when full, shrink when a quarter full.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, grow=lambda cap: max(4, 2 * cap)):\n        self._grow = grow\n        self._cap = self._n = 0\n        self._slots = []          # fixed block: never append to it\n        self.moves = 0            # element copies done by resizes\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return self._n\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        if not 0 &lt;= i &lt; self._n:\n            raise IndexError(i)\n        return self._slots[i]\n\n    def append(self, x):\n        if self._n == self._cap:\n            self._resize(self._grow(self._cap))\n        self._slots[self._n] = x\n        self._n += 1\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if self._n == 0:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from empty array\")\n        self._n -= 1\n        x, self._slots[self._n] = self._slots[self._n], None\n        if self._cap &gt; 4 and self._n &lt;= self._cap // 4:\n            self._resize(self._cap // 2)\n        return x\n\n    def _resize(self, cap):\n        new = [None] * cap\n        for i in range(self._n):\n            new[i] = self._slots[i]\n        self.moves += self._n\n        self._slots, self._cap = new, cap\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>CPython's own growth over the first 17 appends:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Empty list: no block allocated yet\u003c/p>\u003cpre>size 0   capacity 0   moved so far 0\n[]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Append 1: size 1 exceeds capacity 0, so allocate (1 + 0 + 6) &amp; ~3 = 4 slots\u003c/p>\u003cpre>size 1   capacity \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   moved so far 0\n[a _ _ _]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Appends 2 to 4 fit in the spare slots: O(1) each, no allocation\u003c/p>\u003cpre>size \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   capacity 4   moved so far 0\n[a b c d]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Append 5: full, so grow to (5 + 0 + 6) &amp; ~3 = 8; realloc may move the 4 pointers\u003c/p>\u003cpre>size 5   capacity \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>   moved so far \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\n[a b c d e _ _ _]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Append 9: full again, grow to (9 + 1 + 6) &amp; ~3 = 16, moving up to 8 pointers\u003c/p>\u003cpre>size 9   capacity \u003cmark>16\u003c/mark>   moved so far \u003cmark>12\u003c/mark>\n[a b c d e f g h i _ _ _ _ _ _ _]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Append 17: grow to (17 + 2 + 6) &amp; ~3 = 24, moving up to 16\u003c/p>\u003cpre>size 17   capacity \u003cmark>24\u003c/mark>   moved so far \u003cmark>28\u003c/mark>\n28 moves for 17 appends; the ratio stays bounded (below about 9) however long the list gets\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>b = a\u003c/code> copies nothing: both names refer to one list. \u003ccode>a[:]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list(a)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>a.copy()\u003c/code> are \u003cb>shallow\u003c/b> copies (new block, same element objects); \u003ccode>copy.deepcopy\u003c/code> also copies nested lists.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Passing \u003ccode>a[1:]\u003c/code> down a recursion copies n - 1 references per call and turns O(n) into O(n²). Pass indices instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Amortised is not \"every time\": one append can take O(n). For latency-sensitive code, pre-size (\u003ccode>[None] * n\u003c/code> in Python, \u003ccode>reserve\u003c/code> in C++).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>insert(0, x)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> inside a loop is quadratic; a queue needs \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Starting from \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>, how many reallocations happen during 20 appends, and what is the final capacity?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Four, triggered by appends 1, 5, 9 and 17, giving capacities 4, 8, 16 and 24. The final capacity is 24, which \u003ccode>(sys.getsizeof(a) - 56) // 8\u003c/code> confirms on 64-bit CPython 3.12.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An array grows by 1,000 slots each time it fills. Roughly how many element moves does building 10^6 elements cost, and how does doubling compare?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About n² / (2k) = 10^12 / 2,000 = 5 × 10^8 moves (1,000 resizes copying 1,000, 2,000, ... elements). Doubling moves fewer than 2 × 10^6 in total, so the additive scheme is about 250 times more work and gets worse as n grows.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know what a list stores, how CPython grows it, and why append is O(1) amortised but not O(1) every time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:arrays/in-place-reverse-rotate-and-compact":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In place means you mutate the given list and use O(1) extra space besides it. Three moves cover most problems: \u003cb>swap from both ends\u003c/b> to reverse a range, compose \u003cb>three reversals\u003c/b> to rotate, and keep a \u003cb>write index\u003c/b> \u003ccode>w\u003c/code> with the invariant \"a[:w] is the finished output\" to filter or compact in a single pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reverse\u003c/b> a[lo:hi]: swap the two ends and move both inwards. Invariant: everything outside the shrinking window is already in its final position. That is n / 2 swaps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rotate right by k\u003c/b> means the last k elements move to the front. Write the list as A B with B of length k. Reversing everything gives rev(B) rev(A); reversing each part again gives B A. Three passes, about n swaps, no buffer. Reduce k modulo n first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compact\u003c/b> (filter in place): a read index r visits every element and a write index w ≤ r marks the end of the output. Invariant: a[:w] holds, in order, the kept elements of a[:r]. Finish with \u003ccode>del a[w:]\u003c/code>, or return w if the array has a fixed size.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Next permutation\u003c/b> reuses reversal. Find the rightmost i with a[i] &lt; a[i + 1]: the suffix after i is non-increasing, so it is already the largest arrangement of its values. Swap a[i] with the rightmost suffix element larger than it (the suffix stays non-increasing), then reverse the suffix to make it as small as possible. If no such i exists, the array is the last permutation: reverse all of it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The output as workspace.\u003c/b> When the answer is itself an array, it does not count as extra space. Fill it left to right with one fact (say, a running product of everything to the left), then sweep right to left with one running variable for the other side.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>What Python gives you.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>a.reverse()\u003c/code> runs in C, in place. \u003ccode>a[::-1]\u003c/code> builds a new list; \u003ccode>reversed(a)\u003c/code> is a lazy iterator. \u003ccode>a[:] = expr\u003c/code> replaces the contents of the same list object, so callers holding a reference see the change, but it builds the right-hand side first (O(n) extra space).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def reverse(a, lo=0, hi=None):\n    \"\"\"Reverse a[lo:hi] in place.\"\"\"\n    if hi is None:\n        hi = len(a)\n    hi -= 1\n    while lo &lt; hi:\n        a[lo], a[hi] = a[hi], a[lo]\n        lo, hi = lo + 1, hi - 1\n\n\ndef rotate_right(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Move the last k elements to the front with O(1) extra space.\"\"\"\n    n = len(a)\n    if n:\n        k %= n\n        reverse(a)\n        reverse(a, 0, k)\n        reverse(a, k, n)\n\n\ndef compact(a, keep):\n    \"\"\"Keep the elements x with keep(x) true, in order, in place; return the new length.\"\"\"\n    w = 0\n    for r in range(len(a)):\n        if keep(a[r]):\n            a[w] = a[r]\n            w += 1\n    del a[w:]\n    return w\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rotate right by k = 3: the result must be 5 6 7 followed by 1 2 3 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index  0  1  2  3  4  5  6\na      1  2  3  4  5  6  7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse everything: rev(A B) = rev(B) rev(A)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index  0  1  2  3  4  5  6\na      \u003cmark>7  6  5  4  3  2  1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse the first k = 3 elements: rev(rev(B)) = B\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index  0  1  2  3  4  5  6\na      \u003cmark>5  6  7\u003c/mark>  4  3  2  1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse the last n - k = 4 elements: rev(rev(A)) = A; done with about n swaps and no buffer\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index  0  1  2  3  4  5  6\na      5  6  7  \u003cmark>1  2  3  4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Next permutation of [1, 3, 5, 4, 2]: the rightmost ascent is at i = 1 (3 &lt; 5); the rightmost suffix value larger than 3 is 4, so swapping gives [1, 4, 5, 3, 2]; reversing the suffix [5, 3, 2] gives [1, 4, 2, 3, 5].\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>a = a[::-1]\u003c/code> inside a function rebinds the local name and the caller's list is unchanged. Mutate with \u003ccode>a.reverse()\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>a[:] = ...\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rotating with k ≥ n, or an empty list: reduce with \u003ccode>k %= n\u003c/code> and guard \u003ccode>n == 0\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>k % 0\u003c/code> raises).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Removing while iterating (\u003ccode>for x in a: if bad(x): a.remove(x)\u003c/code>) skips the element after each removal and costs O(n²). Compact instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In next permutation the swap partner must be the rightmost larger value; any other choice can leave the suffix out of order, and the final reverse no longer yields the smallest suffix.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What are the next permutations of [2, 3, 1] and of [3, 2, 1]?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[3, 1, 2]: the rightmost ascent is at i = 0 (2 &lt; 3); the rightmost suffix value larger than 2 is 3, so swapping gives [3, 2, 1], and reversing the suffix [2, 1] gives [3, 1, 2]. [3, 2, 1] has no ascent, so it is the last permutation and wraps to [1, 2, 3].\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to reverse, rotate and compact an array in place, and the invariant that proves each pass correct.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:arrays/the-array-as-its-own-index":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When n values all lie in 1..n, the array already has one slot per possible value: slot v - 1 can record a fact about v. \u003cb>Sign marking\u003c/b> negates a[v - 1] to say \"v was seen\" (abs recovers the value); \u003cb>cyclic sort\u003c/b> swaps every v into slot v - 1. Either finds missing and repeated values in O(n) time and O(1) extra space, where a set would need O(n) memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why it works.\u003c/b> A seen-set over 1..n is a boolean array of n entries. When the input is an array of length n with values in 1..n, its own sign bits (or its own positions) can play that boolean array, so the extra memory disappears. You give up the original order or must restore the signs afterwards.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sign marking.\u003c/b> For each x, look at slot \u003ccode>abs(x) - 1\u003c/code>: if it is already negative, x is a repeat; otherwise negate it. Afterwards, every slot i that is still positive means i + 1 never appeared.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cyclic sort.\u003c/b> For each i, while a[i] holds a value v in range whose home slot v - 1 does not already hold v, swap it home. Invariant: a value sitting in its home slot never moves again. Every swap puts one value home for good, so there are at most n swaps in total and the nested loop is O(n) overall, an amortised argument. Afterwards a[i] == i + 1 for every value that occurs; the slots that disagree hold the repeats and mark the missing values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Out-of-range values\u003c/b> (zero, negatives, values above n) have no home slot; they stay wherever the swaps leave them. When the answer can be outside the input (the smallest value not present, say), first ask which range the answer must lie in.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Alternatives.\u003c/b> A set: O(n) time and O(n) memory, and it does not mutate the input. Sorting: O(n log n). For exactly one missing number, a sum or XOR identity works without touching the array (the bits module covers XOR tricks).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def marks(a):\n    \"\"\"Values in 1..n. Return (repeats, missing) with sign marks, then restore a.\"\"\"\n    repeats = []\n    for x in a:\n        i = abs(x) - 1\n        if a[i] &lt; 0:\n            repeats.append(i + 1)      # a value seen k times is reported k - 1 times\n        else:\n            a[i] = -a[i]\n    missing = [i + 1 for i, x in enumerate(a) if x &gt; 0]\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        a[i] = abs(a[i])\n    return repeats, missing\n\n\ndef cyclic_sort(a):\n    \"\"\"Move every value v in 1..n to index v - 1; out-of-range values stay where they land.\"\"\"\n    n = len(a)\n    for i in range(n):\n        while 1 &lt;= a[i] &lt;= n and a[a[i] - 1] != a[i]:\n            j = a[i] - 1               # compute the target first (see Pitfalls)\n            a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Cyclic sort of [2, 5, 1, 2, 4] (n = 5). i = 0: value 2 belongs at index 1, which holds 5, so swap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index  0  1  2  3  4\na      \u003cmark>2  5\u003c/mark>  1  2  4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Still i = 0: value 5 belongs at index 4, which holds 4, so swap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>  2  1  2  \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Still i = 0: value 4 belongs at index 3, which holds 2, so swap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  2  1  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Still i = 0: value 2 belongs at index 1, which already holds 2: a repeat, stop. i = 1 is home\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      \u003cmark>2  2\u003c/mark>  1  4  5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2: value 1 belongs at index 0, which holds 2, so swap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  2  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  4  5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Still i = 2: the 2 that arrived belongs at index 1, which already holds 2, so stop; i = 3 and i = 4 are home\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a      1  2  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  4  5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Final scan: index 2 should hold 3 but holds 2, so 3 is missing and 2 is repeated (4 swaps in all, never more than n)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index  0  1  2  3  4\na      1  2  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  4  5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The tuple-swap trap.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>a[i], a[a[i] - 1] = a[a[i] - 1], a[i]\u003c/code> evaluates the right side first, then assigns targets left to right, so the second target index uses the \u003ci>new\u003c/i> a[i]. On [2, 1] it leaves the list unchanged and the while loop never ends. Compute \u003ccode>j\u003c/code> first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Testing \u003ccode>j != i\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>a[j] != a[i]\u003c/code> loops forever on duplicates: two equal values keep swapping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After sign marking, read values with \u003ccode>abs()\u003c/code>; a slot may already be negative when you reach it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Zero cannot carry a sign mark. Shift values or treat out-of-range values separately.\u003c/li>\u003cli>These methods mutate the input. Restore it (as \u003ccode>marks\u003c/code> does) or say so in the contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run \u003ccode>marks\u003c/code> on [3, 1, 3, 4, 1] (n = 5). What does it return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>([3, 1], [2, 5]). The marks go to slots 2, 0, then 3 is a repeat, slot 3, then 1 is a repeat; slots 1 and 4 stay positive, so 2 and 5 are missing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to make an array's own slots record what has been seen, and why cyclic sort is O(n) despite its nested loop.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:arrays/strings-as-character-arrays":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A Python \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> is an \u003cb>immutable array of Unicode code points\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>s[i]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>len(s)\u003c/code> are O(1), but every change builds a new string. Edit text as a list and \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code> it once; count lowercase letters in a 26-slot list indexed by \u003ccode>ord(ch) - ord(\"a\")\u003c/code>; and remember that \u003ccode>len\u003c/code> counts code points, not the characters a reader sees.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Storage (PEP 393).\u003c/b> CPython stores each string with 1, 2 or 4 bytes per code point, chosen by its widest character, so indexing stays O(1). On 3.12, \u003ccode>sys.getsizeof\u003c/code> reports 41 bytes for \u003ccode>\"\"\u003c/code>, 141 for 100 ASCII letters, 258 for 100 euro signs and 460 for 100 emoji.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Immutability.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>s[i] = \"x\"\u003c/code> raises \u003ccode>TypeError\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>s += t\u003c/code> must produce a new string, and in general n concatenations copy 1 + 2 + ... + n characters: O(n²). CPython hides this when nothing else references \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> (it resizes in place), but any second reference, another interpreter, or a loop that keeps old strings brings the quadratic cost back. \u003ccode>\"\".join(parts)\u003c/code> sums the lengths, allocates once and copies once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slices copy\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>s[i:j]\u003c/code> is O(j - i), and \u003ccode>s[::-1]\u003c/code> is O(n). Methods such as \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>strip\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>lower\u003c/code> return new strings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The hash is cached.\u003c/b> A string computes its hash once and stores it, so repeated dictionary lookups with the same string object do not rehash it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting.\u003c/b> For lowercase English letters, \u003ccode>[0] * 26\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>ord(ch) - 97\u003c/code> is a direct-address table: no hashing, and comparing two tables is O(26). Use \u003ccode>collections.Counter\u003c/code> when the alphabet is large or unknown.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bytes.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code> is immutable; \u003ccode>bytearray\u003c/code> is a mutable byte array with in-place \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code>, the right buffer for byte-level protocols and ASCII-only editing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unicode caveats.\u003c/b> \"é\" can be one code point or two (\"e\" plus a combining accent): they look identical but compare unequal until you \u003ccode>unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", s)\u003c/code>. Caseless comparison needs \u003ccode>casefold()\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>\"ß\".casefold()\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>\"ss\"\u003c/code>). An emoji with a skin tone has \u003ccode>len\u003c/code> 2, a family emoji 5, and \u003ccode>s[::-1]\u003c/code> can split them. When a problem says \"lowercase English letters\", it is telling you the 26-slot trick is safe.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def letter_counts(s):\n    \"\"\"Counts of 'a'..'z' in s; s must contain only lowercase English letters.\"\"\"\n    counts = [0] * 26\n    for ch in s:\n        counts[ord(ch) - ord(\"a\")] += 1\n    return counts\n\n\ndef shift_letters(s, k):\n    \"\"\"Caesar shift by k: collect characters in a list, join once.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for ch in s:\n        out.append(chr((ord(ch) - ord(\"a\") + k) % 26 + ord(\"a\")))\n    return \"\".join(out)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>letter_counts(\"listen\")\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>letter_counts(\"silent\")\u003c/code> both have a 1 in the slots for e, i, l, n, s and t, so the two words are anagrams: O(n + 26) instead of comparing sorted copies in O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003cp>Timing three ways of building a string of n characters, one run of CPython 3.12 on a shared server (milliseconds; doubling n should double a linear cost and quadruple a quadratic one):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>n\u003c/th>\u003cth>\u003ccode>s += \"x\"\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>\u003ccode>s += \"x\"\u003c/code> with a second reference to \u003ccode>s\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>\u003ccode>\"\".join(parts)\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>54\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>100,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>12\u003c/td>\u003ctd>352\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>200,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>21\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,829\u003c/td>\u003ctd>15\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The plain loop looks linear only because of the in-place optimisation; one extra reference (\u003ccode>t = s\u003c/code> before the \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code>) makes every step copy the whole string, and the cost grows at least fourfold per doubling.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>ord(ch) - 97\u003c/code> on input with capitals, digits or accents indexes the wrong slot or raises \u003ccode>IndexError\u003c/code>; check the stated alphabet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>sorted(s)\u003c/code> returns a list of characters; use \u003ccode>\"\".join(sorted(s))\u003c/code> when you need a string.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Calling \u003ccode>s.replace\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>s.find\u003c/code> or slicing inside a loop over the same long string repeats O(n) work each time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>len(s)\u003c/code> is not the number of visible characters, and reversing a string with combining marks or emoji modifiers can corrupt it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can \u003ccode>for w in words: line += w + \" \"\u003c/code> be quadratic even though it is often fast on CPython, and what is the portable fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> may build a new string and copy everything so far, which is O(L²) in the total length L. CPython often avoids the copy by resizing in place when \u003ccode>line\u003c/code> has no other reference, but that is an implementation detail. \u003ccode>\" \".join(words)\u003c/code> is O(L) everywhere.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how Python stores strings, how to build and count them in linear time, and which Unicode facts break naive indexing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:arrays/grids-row-major-indexing-neighbours-and-traversa":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A grid is an array of rows. In \u003cb>row-major\u003c/b> order, cell (r, c) of an R x C grid is flat index \u003ccode>r * C + c\u003c/code>, and flat index k is cell \u003ccode>(k // C, k % C)\u003c/code>. Move with a \u003cb>direction array\u003c/b> and one bounds check, peel a spiral with four shrinking boundaries, and recognise diagonals by an invariant: \u003ccode>r - c\u003c/code> is constant along a top-left to bottom-right diagonal, \u003ccode>r + c\u003c/code> along an anti-diagonal.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lists of lists.\u003c/b> Each row is its own list object, so \u003ccode>g[r][c]\u003c/code> is two pointer hops and rows may differ in length. \u003ccode>[[0] * C] * R\u003c/code> holds R references to one row: write one cell and the whole column changes. Build \u003ccode>[[0] * C for _ in range(R)]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>NumPy and C\u003c/b> store a 2D array as one block (row-major, \u003ccode>order=\"C\"\u003c/code>, by default) with strides: element (r, c) sits at byte offset \u003ccode>r * strides[0] + c * strides[1]\u003c/code>. Scanning row by row reads consecutive memory, so in C and NumPy the last index should vary fastest.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative indices wrap.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>g[-1]\u003c/code> is the last row, so a neighbour at r - 1 = -1 silently reads the wrong cell instead of failing. Check \u003ccode>0 &lt;= nr &lt; R and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; C\u003c/code> every time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Directions.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>DIRS4 = ((0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1), (-1, 0))\u003c/code> lists east, south, west, north, so turning right is \u003ccode>d = (d + 1) % 4\u003c/code>. Add the four diagonals for 8-neighbourhoods.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spiral.\u003c/b> Invariant: the unvisited cells are exactly the rectangle rows top..bottom, columns left..right. Walk its top row, then its right column; walk the bottom row only if rows remain and the left column only if columns remain, shrinking one boundary after each side.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Diagonals.\u003c/b> Group cells by \u003ccode>r - c\u003c/code> (R + C - 1 groups) to check or sort each diagonal; group by \u003ccode>r + c\u003c/code> to walk anti-diagonals, reversing every other one for a zigzag.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Transpose\u003c/b> of a non-square grid makes a new C x R grid: \u003ccode>[list(col) for col in zip(*g)]\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>zip\u003c/code> yields tuples).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>DIRS4 = ((0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1), (-1, 0))           # E, S, W, N: clockwise\n\n\ndef neighbours(r, c, R, C, dirs=DIRS4):\n    for dr, dc in dirs:\n        nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n        if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; R and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; C:\n            yield nr, nc\n\n\ndef spiral(g):\n    \"\"\"Cells of g in clockwise spiral order from the top-left corner.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    if not g or not g[0]:\n        return out\n    top, bottom, left, right = 0, len(g) - 1, 0, len(g[0]) - 1\n    while top &lt;= bottom and left &lt;= right:\n        for c in range(left, right + 1):\n            out.append(g[top][c])\n        top += 1\n        for r in range(top, bottom + 1):\n            out.append(g[r][right])\n        right -= 1\n        if top &lt;= bottom:\n            for c in range(right, left - 1, -1):\n                out.append(g[bottom][c])\n            bottom -= 1\n        if left &lt;= right:\n            for r in range(bottom, top - 1, -1):\n                out.append(g[r][left])\n            left += 1\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">3 x 4 grid, unvisited rectangle rows 0..2, columns 0..3. Walk the top row, then top = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>top 0  bottom 2  left 0  right 3      out: a b c d\n      c0 c1 c2 c3\n  r0   \u003cmark>a  b  c  d\u003c/mark>\n  r1   e  f  g  h\n  r2   i  j  k  l\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Walk the right column, rows 1..2, then right = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>top 1  bottom 2  left 0  right 3      out: ... h l\n      c0 c1 c2 c3\n  r0   .  .  .  .\n  r1   e  f  g  \u003cmark>h\u003c/mark>\n  r2   i  j  k  \u003cmark>l\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rows remain (1 &lt;= 2): walk the bottom row right to left, columns 2..0, then bottom = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>top 1  bottom 2  left 0  right 2      out: ... k j i\n      c0 c1 c2 c3\n  r0   .  .  .  .\n  r1   e  f  g  .\n  r2   \u003cmark>i  j  k\u003c/mark>  .\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Columns remain (0 &lt;= 2): walk the left column upwards, rows 1..1, then left = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>top 1  bottom 1  left 0  right 2      out: ... e\n      c0 c1 c2 c3\n  r0   .  .  .  .\n  r1   \u003cmark>e\u003c/mark>  f  g  .\n  r2   .  .  .  .\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second lap, rectangle rows 1..1, columns 1..2: top row gives f g, then top = 2 and right = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>top 2  bottom 1  left 1  right 1      out: ... f g\n      c0 c1 c2 c3\n  r1   .  \u003cmark>f  g\u003c/mark>  .\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">top 2 &gt; bottom 1: the bottom-row walk is skipped (without the check, f would be emitted twice); the left column is empty; the loop ends\u003c/p>\u003cpre>out: a b c d h l k j i e f g   (12 cells, each exactly once)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>[[0] * C] * R\u003c/code> aliasing, described above; it passes tests that only read the grid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Swapping R and C: \u003ccode>len(g)\u003c/code> is the row count and \u003ccode>len(g[0])\u003c/code> the column count. Test with a non-square grid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Dropping the two re-checks in the spiral emits the middle row or column twice when the remaining rectangle is one cell thick.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty grid (\u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>[[]]\u003c/code>): \u003ccode>g[0]\u003c/code> raises on \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>, so guard first.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In a 3 x 5 grid, which cell has flat index 11, which flat index does cell (2, 3) have, and which cells lie on the anti-diagonal r + c = 3?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Cell (2, 1), since 11 // 5 = 2 and 11 % 5 = 1. Cell (2, 3) is 2 * 5 + 3 = 13. The anti-diagonal r + c = 3 holds (0, 3), (1, 2) and (2, 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to index a grid with row-major arithmetic, walk it safely, and produce spiral and diagonal orders from boundary and diagonal invariants.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:arrays/transforming-matrices-in-place":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build a transform from simple in-place passes: a clockwise quarter turn is a \u003cb>transpose followed by reversing each row\u003c/b>. When every new value depends on old neighbours, do not copy the grid: keep \u003cb>both states in each cell\u003c/b> (bit 0 old, bit 1 new), or store flags in cells whose information you no longer need, then finalise in a second pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Where a cell goes.\u003c/b> A clockwise turn sends (r, c) to (c, n - 1 - r). Transposing sends (r, c) to (c, r); reversing each row then sends (c, r) to (c, n - 1 - r). Counter-clockwise: transpose, then reverse the order of the rows (\u003ccode>m.reverse()\u003c/code>). Half turn: reverse the rows and every row.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Transpose in place\u003c/b> works only for square matrices (the shape changes otherwise) and must swap only the upper triangle, c &gt; r: swapping every pair twice puts everything back.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Layer by layer\u003c/b> is the alternative: rotate four cells at a time around each ring. Same O(n²), one pass, more index arithmetic to get wrong.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two states per cell.\u003c/b> In a synchronous update (every next state uses the current neighbours, as in the Game of Life), store the next state in bit 1: read neighbours with \u003ccode>cell &amp; 1\u003c/code>, set \u003ccode>cell |= 2\u003c/code> when the cell will be alive, and after the whole pass shift every cell right by one. Any encoding works if the old state stays recoverable until the last cell is computed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Borrowed storage.\u003c/b> If you need one flag per row and per column in O(1) extra space, some cells of the matrix itself can hold the flags once their own information is saved, provided the final pass never overwrites a flag before reading it. Working out which cells, and in which order to apply them, is the exercise.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python reality.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>m[:] = [list(row) for row in zip(*m[::-1])]\u003c/code> rotates clockwise in one line but builds a whole new grid: fine in production, not when O(1) extra space is required. NumPy's \u003ccode>np.rot90(a, -1)\u003c/code> returns a view (it only rearranges strides), so it costs O(1) until you copy it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def rotate_clockwise(m):\n    \"\"\"Rotate a square matrix 90 degrees clockwise in place.\"\"\"\n    n = len(m)\n    for r in range(n):\n        for c in range(r + 1, n):              # upper triangle only\n            m[r][c], m[c][r] = m[c][r], m[r][c]\n    for row in m:\n        row.reverse()\n\n\ndef commit_next_states(g):\n    \"\"\"After a pass that set bit 1 (g[r][c] |= 2) for every cell alive next, make it current.\"\"\"\n    for row in g:\n        for c in range(len(row)):\n            row[c] &gt;&gt;= 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: the left column 1 4 7 must become the top row, read bottom to top: 7 4 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>1  2  3\n4  5  6\n7  8  9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Transpose: swap m[r][c] with m[c][r] for c &gt; r (three swaps); the diagonal 1 5 9 stays put\u003c/p>\u003cpre>1  \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>\n\u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  5  \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\n\u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>  9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse each row: (c, r) moves to (c, n - 1 - r), completing (r, c) -&gt; (c, n - 1 - r)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>7  4  1\u003c/mark>\n\u003cmark>8  5  2\u003c/mark>\n\u003cmark>9  6  3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two-state cells in the Game of Life: a live cell (1) with two live neighbours survives, so it becomes 3 (binary 11); a dead cell (0) with three live neighbours is born, so it becomes 2 (binary 10). Neighbours keep reading bit 0, which still holds the old state. After the pass, \u003ccode>&gt;&gt;= 1\u003c/code> turns 3 and 2 into 1, and 1 and 0 into 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Transposing both triangles swaps each pair twice: nothing changes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Testing \u003ccode>cell == 1\u003c/code> after encoding: a live cell may already be 3. Read \u003ccode>cell &amp; 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Writing a final value before every neighbour has read the old one: exactly the bug the two-state trick prevents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With borrowed flag cells, applying the flags of the borrowed row or column first destroys flags you still need.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which two in-place passes rotate a square matrix 90 degrees counter-clockwise?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Transpose, then reverse the order of the rows (\u003ccode>m.reverse()\u003c/code>). The transpose sends (r, c) to (c, r) and reversing the row order sends row c to row n - 1 - c, so (r, c) ends at (n - 1 - c, r), which is the counter-clockwise position. Reversing each row first and then transposing also works.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to compose rotations from transposes and reversals, and how to update a grid synchronously without copying it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:arrays/build-a-dynamic-array-and-measure-growth-factors":"\u003cp>You will extend the \u003ccode>DynArray\u003c/code> from the first recap, prove it correct against \u003ccode>list\u003c/code> with a random tester, and then measure what the growth factor buys and costs. Predict every number before you run it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or later, standard library only. Save everything in \u003ccode>dynarray_lab.py\u003c/code>, starting with the \u003ccode>DynArray\u003c/code> class from the first recap.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Add \u003ccode>__setitem__(i, x)\u003c/code> with the same bounds check as \u003ccode>__getitem__\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>insert(i, x)\u003c/code> for 0 &lt;= i &lt;= len: grow if full, shift a[i:] one slot right starting from the end, then write x. Count shifted elements in \u003ccode>self.moves\u003c/code> too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stress-test it against a plain list. A mismatch prints the seed, so you can replay the failing run:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\n\ndef stress(runs=300, ops=300):\n    for seed in range(runs):\n        rng = random.Random(seed)\n        a, model = DynArray(), []\n        for _ in range(ops):\n            op = rng.choice(\"aaapis\")\n            if op == \"a\":\n                x = rng.randint(0, 99)\n                a.append(x)\n                model.append(x)\n            elif op == \"p\" and model:\n                assert a.pop() == model.pop(), seed\n            elif op == \"i\":\n                i, x = rng.randint(0, len(model)), rng.randint(0, 99)\n                a.insert(i, x)\n                model.insert(i, x)\n            elif op == \"s\" and model:\n                i, x = rng.randrange(len(model)), rng.randint(0, 99)\n                a[i] = x\n                model[i] = x\n            assert len(a) == len(model) and [a[i] for i in range(len(a))] == model, seed\n            assert a._n &lt;= a._cap and (a._cap &lt;= 4 or a._n &gt; a._cap // 4), seed\n    print(\"stress test passed\")\n\n\nstress()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Measure moves per append and the average share of unused capacity for three geometric factors and for additive growth. Before running, write down the band you expect from the recap: between 1 / (r - 1) and r / (r - 1) moves per append.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def factor(r):\n    return lambda cap: max(4, cap + 1, int(cap * r))\n\n\ndef profile(grow, n):\n    a, wasted = DynArray(grow), 0.0\n    for i in range(n):\n        a.append(i)\n        wasted += (a._cap - len(a)) / a._cap\n    return a.moves / n, wasted / n\n\n\nfor name, grow in [(\"x2\", factor(2)), (\"x1.5\", factor(1.5)), (\"x1.125\", factor(1.125)),\n                   (\"+16\", lambda cap: cap + 16)]:\n    sizes = (10**4, 10**5) if name == \"+16\" else (10**4, 10**5, 10**6)\n    for n in sizes:\n        moves, wasted = profile(grow, n)\n        print(f\"{name:7} n={n:&gt;9,}  moves/append={moves:8.2f}  unused={wasted:5.1%}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Reproduce CPython's growth from the outside: append 100 items to a real list and record the capacity whenever it changes, using \u003ccode>(sys.getsizeof(a) - sys.getsizeof([])) // 8\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Time one million appends to \u003ccode>DynArray\u003c/code> and to \u003ccode>list\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>time.perf_counter\u003c/code>, three runs each, and report the best.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Measure memory with \u003ccode>tracemalloc\u003c/code>: one million distinct floats in a \u003ccode>list\u003c/code>, in \u003ccode>array('d')\u003c/code>, and in your \u003ccode>DynArray\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional: change the shrink rule in \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code> from a quarter to a half (\u003ccode>self._n &lt;= self._cap // 2\u003c/code>), then alternate append and pop 10,000 times on an array of 4,096 items whose capacity is exactly 4,096 (build it by appending 4,096 items with doubling). Count \u003ccode>moves\u003c/code> under both rules.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>These numbers come from CPython 3.12 on a shared Linux server; yours will differ in the second digit, not the first.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>The stress test passes. If it fails, the usual culprits are shifting from the front in \u003ccode>insert\u003c/code> (it overwrites before copying) and forgetting to grow before inserting into a full block.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Moves per append at n = 10^6 land inside the predicted band: about 1.05 for x2 (band 1 to 2), 2.1 for x1.5 (band 2 to 3) and 8.7 for x1.125 (band 8 to 9). They wobble with n because the last resize may or may not have just happened. Additive +16 gives about 312 moves per append at 10^4 and about 3,100 at 10^5: ten times the elements, a hundred times the total work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Average unused capacity is roughly 26 to 29% for x2, 17 to 18% for x1.5 and under 6% for x1.125: the memory you save by growing slowly is paid for in moves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>CPython's capacities come out as 4, 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, 52, 64, 76, 92, 108, matching the comment in \u003ccode>listobject.c\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A million \u003ccode>DynArray.append\u003c/code> calls take about 0.45 s against about 0.06 s for \u003ccode>list.append\u003c/code> in the same Python loop, so roughly 5 to 10 times slower. The shape matches; the constant is your resize and bookkeeping code running in the interpreter instead of C.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A million floats take about 32 MB as a \u003ccode>list\u003c/code> and about the same in \u003ccode>DynArray\u003c/code> (both store references to 24-byte float objects), against 8 MB in \u003ccode>array('d')\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the half-full shrink rule, alternating append and pop at the boundary triggers a resize on every operation: 4,096 moves per operation, about 41 million in total, against a single resize (4,096 moves in all) with the quarter rule. This is why real dynamic arrays shrink late.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your measurement for x1.5 says 2.77 moves per append at n = 100,000 but 2.10 at 1,000,000. Is something wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. The average depends on where n falls relative to the last resize: just after a resize the array has paid for a large copy that later appends have not yet amortised. Both values lie inside the band from 1 / (r - 1) = 2 to r / (r - 1) = 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:566":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repackage a rectangular grid into a requested number of rows and columns, preserving its row-by-row reading order. If the requested shape cannot hold exactly the same number of entries, return the original grid. For example, reshaping \u003ccode>[[1, 2], [3, 4]]\u003c/code> to two rows of two columns changes no values; reshaping it to one row gives \u003ccode>[[1, 2, 3, 4]]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume the input is a nonempty rectangular grid and both requested dimensions are positive. Source dimensions can differ, and cells can contain arbitrary values. A legal reshape needs \u003ccode>rows * cols == m * n\u003c/code>; otherwise it would drop or invent a cell. On failure this implementation returns the original object. On success it creates independent output rows and does not mutate the source.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two grid shapes contain the same cells in the same reading order.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Number the cells 0, 1, 2, ... in reading order. How do you turn that number into a row and column for each shape?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid indexing and traversal orders\u003c/p>\u003cp>If m * n != r * c the reshape is impossible, so return the input. Otherwise flat index k is cell (k // n, k % n) in the source and (k // c, k % c) in the target, so one loop over k copies everything in row-major order.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(m n) for the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First copy all values into a flat list, then consume that list into new rows. This is straightforward and takes O(mn) time, but the temporary flat list adds O(mn) storage beyond the output. The mapping below needs only the output and a few indices.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Number every cell by its position in row-major order. For a width \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, index \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> means row \u003ccode>k // n\u003c/code> and column \u003ccode>k % n\u003c/code>. The same \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, interpreted using the target width, identifies its destination. Both mappings are bijections over the same range, so every input value is copied exactly once to the corresponding output position.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Source [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], target 3 x 2: both shapes contain 6 cells.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 0, 1: source (0,0), (0,1) becomes destination (0,0), (0,1); output starts {{1, 2}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 2, 3: source (0,2), (1,0) becomes destination (1,0), (1,1); second row is {{3, 4}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 4, 5: source (1,1), (1,2) becomes destination (2,0), (2,1); result is [[1, 2], [3, 4], {{5, 6}}].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def reshape(grid, rows, cols):\n    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    if m * n != rows * cols:\n        return grid\n    out = [[None] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    for k in range(m * n):\n        out[k // cols][k % cols] = grid[k // n][k % n]\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are \u003ccode>m*n\u003c/code> assignments, so time is O(mn). The returned grid occupies O(mn) space; auxiliary space apart from it is O(1). The incompatible-shape case returns immediately in O(1) time and space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use the source width to decode source coordinates and the target width for the destination.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Construct rows independently: multiplying an outer list aliases rows and corrupts later writes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A one-row or one-column shape works without a special traversal. The stated contract excludes an empty or ragged input.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Convert a flat row-major buffer to a grid by applying only the destination mapping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a column-major representation, derive coordinates using the height rather than the width.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:448":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An array of length \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> contains values drawn from \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, possibly with repetition. Return all values from that range which do not appear. For \u003ccode>[2, 2, 4, 1]\u003c/code>, the missing values are \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code>. The returned list is allowed; the working storage beyond it should be constant.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each value has a valid home index \u003ccode>value - 1\u003c/code>. The list may be modified; this implementation restores it before returning, so callers retain its original contents. The sign trick relies on every original value being positive and within range. An empty list naturally returns an empty result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Values lie in 1..n in an array of length n, and the follow-up asks for no extra space.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each value names a slot. How can you mark that slot as visited without losing the number stored in it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Array as its own index\u003c/p>\u003cp>For every x, negate slot abs(x) - 1 if it is still positive. Afterwards slot i is positive exactly when i + 1 never appeared; abs() recovers values that were marked before you reached them.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space besides the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each candidate from 1 to n, scan the array for it. This takes O(n²) time and constant working storage. A set makes membership fast in expected O(n) time but needs O(n) extra storage.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the sign at each home index as a visited bit. For each value \u003ccode>x\u003c/code>, make the value at \u003ccode>abs(x) - 1\u003c/code> negative. The magnitude remains the original value, so later visits can still recover it. At the end, a positive entry at index \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> means nobody visited the home slot of \u003ccode>i + 1\u003c/code>; a negative entry means that value occurred. Negating only positive entries makes repeat visits idempotent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [2, 2, 4, 1]: all home-slot signs are positive.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 2 then abs(-2) = 2: mark index 1 negative, then leave it negative; array is [2, {{-2}}, 4, 1].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 4: mark index 3; read abs(-1) = 1 at index 3 and mark index 0. Array is {{[-2, -2, 4, -1]}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Only index 2 is positive, so output [{{3}}]. Restore all magnitudes to recover [2, 2, 4, 1].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def missing_numbers(a):\n    for x in a:\n        i = abs(x) - 1\n        if a[i] &gt; 0:\n            a[i] = -a[i]\n    missing = [i + 1 for i, x in enumerate(a) if x &gt; 0]\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        a[i] = abs(a[i])\n    return missing\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Three linear passes cost O(n) time. Beyond the returned list, only a few indices are stored, so auxiliary space is O(1). Restoring signs does not require a separate copy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Using a signed value directly as an index is wrong after earlier visits have marked it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Toggling the sign on every occurrence loses the mark after an even number of visits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Duplicates can occur many times; no assumption of a single repeated number is needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not use this encoding on zero or negative original values: zero cannot encode a sign bit.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Report repeated visits when an already negative home slot is encountered; specify whether repeated copies are emitted once or multiple times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use cyclic placement if reordering the array is permitted instead of sign changes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:645":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A length-\u003ccode>n\u003c/code> collection should contain each integer from \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> once, but one value has been replaced by another value already present. Return \u003ccode>[duplicate, missing]\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>[1, 3, 3, 4]\u003c/code>, the answer is \u003ccode>[3, 2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume \u003ccode>n &gt;= 2\u003c/code>, all entries are in \u003ccode>1..n\u003c/code>, and exactly one value appears twice while exactly one is absent. Thus every value has a usable home index, there is exactly one repeated visit, and precisely one slot remains unmarked. This function restores the input after temporarily changing its signs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A permutation of 1..n where one value was overwritten by a copy of another, and you must name both.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Mark the slot of each value as you go. What do you see when you arrive at a slot that is already marked?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Array as its own index\u003c/p>\u003cp>With sign marking, the value whose slot is already negative is the duplicate; after the pass, the one slot still positive gives the missing value (index + 1). Sum identities also work: the duplicate minus the missing value equals sum(a) - n(n + 1)/2.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every candidate, count its occurrences with a full scan. A count of two identifies the duplicate and a count of zero identifies the missing value. This needs O(n²) time and O(1) workspace; a frequency table reduces time to O(n) but uses O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use slot \u003ccode>v - 1\u003c/code> to record whether \u003ccode>v\u003c/code> has been seen. A negative slot means that its value has appeared before. Reading the absolute magnitude preserves the original number even if its position was marked earlier. The second encounter with the duplicate finds a negative slot, while the sole positive slot after the pass corresponds to the missing value. Restoring signs afterwards preserves the caller's data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [1, 3, 3, 4]. Reading 1 marks index 0: {{[-1, 3, 3, 4]}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 3 and mark index 2: [-1, 3, {{-3}}, 4].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read abs(-3) = 3: index 2 is already negative, so duplicate = {{3}}. Read 4 and mark index 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The only positive slot is index 1, so missing = {{2}}. Restore [1, 3, 3, 4] and return [3, 2].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def set_mismatch(a):\n    duplicate = None\n    for x in a:\n        value = abs(x)\n        i = value - 1\n        if a[i] &lt; 0:\n            duplicate = value\n        else:\n            a[i] = -a[i]\n    missing = next(i + 1 for i, x in enumerate(a) if x &gt; 0)\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        a[i] = abs(a[i])\n    return [duplicate, missing]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Marking, finding the positive slot, and restoring each require at most one linear pass: O(n) time. The workspace is O(1), excluding the fixed-size returned pair.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return duplicate first, not the remaining positive index first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The number read from the array may already have been negated; take its absolute value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not use the code on inputs outside the promised range or on multiple errors; its result relies on the stated uniqueness guarantee.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The missing slot identifies a value by index, not by the magnitude currently stored there.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track frequencies in a dictionary if the domain is not a dense interval and extra space is allowed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sum and squared-sum differences yield two equations for the duplicate and missing values; watch integer overflow in fixed-width languages.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:31":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rearrange a list of comparable values into its immediate next lexicographic ordering, using the same elements and modifying the original list. If no larger ordering exists, wrap to the smallest ordering. Equal values are allowed. For \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3]\u003c/code>; for \u003ccode>[3, 2, 1]\u003c/code>, it is \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Lexicographic order compares the first position where two arrangements differ. Therefore the smallest possible increase changes a position as far right as possible, then makes the suffix as small as possible. The routine works for an empty list and a singleton as well as duplicate values. It returns \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> and mutates the existing list, so references to the original object see the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rearrange into the next larger ordering of the same values, in place, wrapping to the smallest at the end.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at the array from the right. Which suffix is already as large as it can possibly be?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place reverse, rotate and compact\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the rightmost i with a[i] &lt; a[i + 1]; the suffix after it is non-increasing. Swap a[i] with the rightmost suffix element larger than it, then reverse the suffix to make it as small as possible. If there is no such i, reverse the whole array.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Generate every distinct permutation, sort them, find the current arrangement, and take its successor modulo the count. The number of permutations can be factorial in \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, and storing them uses factorial space. This is useful only as a tiny-input oracle, not as the algorithm.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan from the right for the first index \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>a[i] &lt; a[i+1]\u003c/code>. Everything to its right is non-increasing, hence already the greatest arrangement of those suffix elements. No change confined to that suffix can increase the whole list. If no such pivot exists, the entire list is greatest and reversing it yields the smallest.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Otherwise change the pivot to the smallest suffix value strictly greater than it. Because the suffix is non-increasing, its rightmost value greater than the pivot is precisely that choice. After swapping, the suffix remains non-increasing: the displaced pivot fits between the values to its left, which were at least the chosen value, and those to its right, which were at most the pivot. Reverse the suffix to obtain the smallest completion. These two choices ensure that no valid ordering lies between the old and new lists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [1, 3, 5, 4, 2]. The suffix [5, 4, 2] is non-increasing; its left neighbour 3 is the rightmost ascent.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pivot index 1 holds 3. Scanning right to left, the first value greater than 3 is {{4}} at index 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Swap pivot and successor: [1, {{4}}, 5, {{3}}, 2]. The suffix [5, 3, 2] remains non-increasing.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse that suffix: [1, 4, {{2, 3, 5}}]. This is the least ordering larger than the original.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def next_permutation(a):\n    i = len(a) - 2\n    while i &gt;= 0 and a[i] &gt;= a[i + 1]:\n        i -= 1\n    if i &gt;= 0:\n        j = len(a) - 1\n        while a[j] &lt;= a[i]:\n            j -= 1\n        a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]\n    left, right = i + 1, len(a) - 1\n    while left &lt; right:\n        a[left], a[right] = a[right], a[left]\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each scan and the reversal touches at most n positions, so time is O(n). A fixed number of indices and swaps gives O(1) extra space. The factorial alternative does not share these bounds.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use strict increase at the pivot and a strictly greater swap partner; equal elements do not produce a larger permutation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Picking the leftmost larger suffix value can skip valid intermediate arrangements.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reverse only the suffix after the swap; when no pivot exists, \u003ccode>i = -1\u003c/code> makes the whole list the suffix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorting the suffix also works logically but takes O(n log n) in general and is unnecessary.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Find the previous ordering by reversing both inequality directions and making the suffix maximal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Iterate this operation to enumerate distinct permutations in lexicographic order from the smallest arrangement.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:54":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the entries of a rectangular grid by starting at the upper-left corner, moving right, then repeatedly turning clockwise as the remaining unvisited region narrows. Every cell must appear exactly once. A \u003ccode>2 x 3\u003c/code> grid \u003ccode>[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\u003c/code> yields \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 6, 5, 4]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The grid is rectangular; \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> and grids with zero columns return an empty list. Neither the grid nor its rows are modified. The result requires one entry per cell, so output space is unavoidable. A single row or column is particularly important: after walking one side, an opposite side may have disappeared.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Read every cell of a rectangle once, going round its edge clockwise and moving inwards.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Describe the cells not yet visited as a rectangle. Which boundary moves after you finish each side?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid indexing and traversal orders\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep top, bottom, left and right. Walk the top row and the right column, shrinking those bounds; walk the bottom row only if top &lt;= bottom still holds and the left column only if left &lt;= right, so a one-cell-thick middle is not read twice.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(1) extra space besides the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a visited matrix and a current direction. At each step, mark the current cell, then advance unless the next position is outside the grid or already visited; in that case turn clockwise. This is O(mn) time but needs O(mn) extra visited storage beyond the output. It is also a useful independent way to check the boundary method.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The unvisited cells always form a rectangle bounded by \u003ccode>top\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bottom\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>right\u003c/code>, inclusive. Consume the top row and move \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> down; consume the right column and move \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> left. If a row remains, consume its bottom row backwards and move \u003ccode>bottom\u003c/code> up. If a column remains, consume its left column upwards and move \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> right.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each pass removes exactly one outer edge from the current rectangle. The two checks after removing earlier edges prevent the same remaining row or column being read twice. The invariant is restored with a smaller rectangle, and termination occurs when it is empty; consequently each cell is emitted once in the intended order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grid [[1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]]; bounds rows 0..2, columns 0..2.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Top row emits {{1,2,3}}; top becomes 1. Right column emits {{6,9}}; right becomes 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rows remain: bottom row reversed emits {{8,7}}; bottom becomes 1. Columns remain: left column emits {{4}}; left becomes 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The remaining rectangle is the single cell (1,1). Top emits {{5}}; bounds cross and the checks suppress duplicate edges.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Return [1,2,3,6,9,8,7,4,5].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def spiral_order(grid):\n    if not grid or not grid[0]:\n        return []\n    top, bottom = 0, len(grid) - 1\n    left, right = 0, len(grid[0]) - 1\n    out = []\n    while top &lt;= bottom and left &lt;= right:\n        for c in range(left, right + 1):\n            out.append(grid[top][c])\n        top += 1\n        for r in range(top, bottom + 1):\n            out.append(grid[r][right])\n        right -= 1\n        if top &lt;= bottom:\n            for c in range(right, left - 1, -1):\n                out.append(grid[bottom][c])\n            bottom -= 1\n        if left &lt;= right:\n            for r in range(bottom, top - 1, -1):\n                out.append(grid[r][left])\n            left += 1\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each of the \u003ccode>m*n\u003c/code> cells is appended once: O(mn) time. The output occupies O(mn) space; the four bounds use O(1) additional space. The empty case is constant time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Recheck the surviving row and column before their opposite edges; otherwise the middle strip can be duplicated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A grid with one row, one column, or only one cell still obeys the boundary invariant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Python negative indices wrap, so blindly traversing a crossed boundary can silently read the wrong row.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rectangular input is required; rows of unequal lengths need a different definition of traversal.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Generate a grid by writing consecutive values in spiral order instead of reading them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the starting corner or turn direction by changing the edge order and the direction of each pass.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:498":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>List a rectangular grid's values in alternating anti-diagonals. Starting at the upper-left, the first diagonal goes upward and right, the next downward and left, and directions alternate thereafter. A \u003ccode>2 x 3\u003c/code> grid \u003ccode>[[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]\u003c/code> produces \u003ccode>[1,2,4,5,3,6]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All rows have the same number of columns; an empty grid or zero-column grid produces \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> rows and \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> columns, an anti-diagonal contains positions with the same sum \u003ccode>r + c\u003c/code>, ranging from zero through \u003ccode>m + n - 2\u003c/code>. The input is not modified and the output has \u003ccode>m*n\u003c/code> entries.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Output the cells along anti-diagonals, alternating direction on each one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>What do all cells on one anti-diagonal have in common?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid indexing and traversal orders\u003c/p>\u003cp>Cells with equal r + c form one anti-diagonal, so loop d from 0 to m + n - 2, collect the valid cells of diagonal d from the top row down, and reverse every even d so it reads bottom-left to top-right.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(min(m, n)) extra space per diagonal\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each possible diagonal sum, scan all cells and collect those with the required sum, then order that group according to direction. This is correct but repeats a full grid scan for every diagonal, taking O(mn(m+n)) time. Storing all groups at once takes O(mn) extra space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On diagonal \u003ccode>d\u003c/code>, choose row \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> and compute column \u003ccode>d - r\u003c/code>. Valid rows lie between \u003ccode>max(0, d - (n-1))\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>min(m-1, d)\u003c/code>, inclusive. These bounds ensure both coordinates are in range and enumerate each cell exactly once, since each has exactly one sum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For even \u003ccode>d\u003c/code>, enumerate rows from high to low: that walks upward and right. For odd \u003ccode>d\u003c/code>, enumerate low to high: that walks downward and left. Directly choosing row direction avoids a temporary list for each diagonal. The output order is therefore determined by the diagonal sum first and the alternating row order second.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grid [[1,2,3], [4,5,6]], m = 2, n = 3. Sums run from 0 to 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">d = 0, even: valid rows 0..0, descending gives {{1}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">d = 1, odd: rows 0..1, ascending gives {{2,4}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">d = 2, even: rows 0..1, descending gives {{5,3}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">d = 3, odd: only row 1 is valid, giving {{6}}. Concatenate to get [1,2,4,5,3,6].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def diagonal_order(grid):\n    if not grid or not grid[0]:\n        return []\n    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    out = []\n    for d in range(m + n - 1):\n        lo = max(0, d - n + 1)\n        hi = min(m - 1, d)\n        rows = range(hi, lo - 1, -1) if d % 2 == 0 else range(lo, hi + 1)\n        for r in rows:\n            out.append(grid[r][d - r])\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each diagonal performs constant setup and each cell is appended once. For nonempty dimensions \u003ccode>m+n-1 &lt;= m*n\u003c/code>, so time is O(mn). Output space is O(mn), and auxiliary space beyond it is O(1), including the lazy \u003ccode>range\u003c/code> object.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Confusing \u003ccode>r+c\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>r-c\u003c/code> follows the other family of diagonals.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reversing odd rather than even diagonals produces the wrong initial direction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Calculate bounds using the column count for the lower row bound; test tall and wide grids, not only squares.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A one-row or one-column grid naturally preserves its only possible order.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Emit all anti-diagonals in the same direction by always iterating rows upward.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Group main diagonals by \u003ccode>r-c\u003c/code> to process entries from upper left to lower right.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:48":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turn a square grid clockwise by a quarter turn, modifying the same grid in place. For instance, \u003ccode>[[1,2], [3,4]]\u003c/code> becomes \u003ccode>[[3,1], [4,2]]\u003c/code>. The cell originally at row \u003ccode>r\u003c/code>, column \u003ccode>c\u003c/code> ends at row \u003ccode>c\u003c/code>, column \u003ccode>n-1-r\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The matrix is square and has independently stored rows. The operation must mutate the caller's outer list and existing rows rather than replace them with a separate rotated grid. Cell values themselves can be arbitrary objects; only their positions matter. An empty square or a one-cell square is already rotated.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rotate a square matrix a quarter turn without allocating another matrix.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can the rotation be written as two simpler rearrangements that are each easy to do in place?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place matrix transforms\u003c/p>\u003cp>Transpose by swapping across the main diagonal (upper triangle only), then reverse every row. Together they send (r, c) to (c, n - 1 - r), which is exactly a clockwise quarter turn.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Allocate a second \u003ccode>n x n\u003c/code> grid and copy each source cell to \u003ccode>(c, n-1-r)\u003c/code>, then copy all the values back. This is O(n²) time and O(n²) temporary space. It is an easy reference model but violates the constant-workspace requirement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compose two bijections. First transpose across the main diagonal, mapping \u003ccode>(r,c)\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>(c,r)\u003c/code>. Then reverse each row, sending column \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>n-1-r\u003c/code>. Their composition is the desired mapping \u003ccode>(r,c) -&gt; (c,n-1-r)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>In-place transposition swaps each off-diagonal pair exactly once. Restricting the column loop to \u003ccode>c &gt; r\u003c/code> chooses one member of every pair and leaves diagonal entries alone. Reversing each row then rearranges its entries without another matrix. Since both operations are bijections, they neither lose nor duplicate any cell.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The order of these operations matters: reversing rows before transposing instead gives a counter-clockwise turn. In the clockwise version, an original top-row cell becomes the rightmost entry of its destination row, whereas an original bottom-row cell becomes the leftmost. This provides a quick visual check in addition to the coordinate proof.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [[1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]]. Each original column should become a row read bottom to top.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Transpose the upper triangle: [[1,{{4}},{{7}}], [{{2}},5,{{8}}], [{{3}},{{6}},9]].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse each row: [{{[7,4,1]}}, {{[8,5,2]}}, {{[9,6,3]}}].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For example, original 4 moves (1,0) -&gt; (0,1) by transpose -&gt; (0,1) by row reversal; original 7 moves (2,0) -&gt; (0,2) -&gt; (0,0).\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def rotate_clockwise(matrix):\n    n = len(matrix)\n    for r in range(n):\n        for c in range(r + 1, n):\n            matrix[r][c], matrix[c][r] = matrix[c][r], matrix[r][c]\n    for row in matrix:\n        row.reverse()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Transposition performs \u003ccode>n(n-1)/2\u003c/code> swaps and row reversals together perform O(n²) work, so time is O(n²). Only loop variables and swap temporaries are required: O(1) auxiliary space. Python's list \u003ccode>reverse\u003c/code> modifies the row itself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Swapping both triangles undoes each swap; use only the strict upper triangle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Transpose followed by reversing the order of the rows rotates counter-clockwise, not clockwise.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replacing the whole matrix with a newly constructed expression hides O(n²) space and may break row aliases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A rectangular grid changes shape on a quarter turn and is outside this in-place square contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For a counter-clockwise turn, transpose and reverse the order of rows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rotate four corresponding positions at a time around each concentric layer without a separate transpose pass.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:289":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Update a rectangular board of zeros and ones by one simultaneous generation. Each cell has up to eight surrounding neighbours. A live cell survives with two or three live neighbours; a dead cell becomes live with exactly three. Every other cell is dead in the new generation. Update the board in place.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input is rectangular, with each entry initially exactly \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>1\u003c/code>; rows are independent mutable lists. Off-board positions do not contribute neighbours. All decisions must see the original generation, not a mixture of old and new states. Empty boards and zero-column boards are unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every cell's next state depends on its neighbours' current states, and the update must happen in place.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each cell holds 0 or 1, but an int has room for more. What if a cell could remember its old state and its new one at the same time?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place matrix transforms\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count live neighbours with cell &amp; 1, and set cell |= 2 when the cell is alive in the next generation (2 or 3 neighbours if alive, exactly 3 if dead). After the full pass, shift every cell right by one.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read all neighbours from the original board while writing each new cell into a second grid, then copy that grid back. It runs in O(mn) time because each cell has at most eight neighbours, but consumes O(mn) extra space. The separate-grid version makes a useful independent oracle for this in-place solution.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One integer cell can hold two bits. Keep its old state in bit zero and encode its next state in bit one. During the first pass, count each neighbour with \u003ccode>cell &amp; 1\u003c/code>, which ignores whether that neighbour's next-state bit has already been set. Set bit one if and only if the cell will be alive.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The old generation therefore remains readable throughout the first pass. Once every new state has been computed, shift each cell right once to discard the old bit and expose the new one. The two passes give exactly the same simultaneous result as computing into a separate grid. An originally dead cell that will live is encoded as \u003ccode>2\u003c/code>, and a surviving live cell is encoded as \u003ccode>3\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [[0,0,0], [1,1,1], [0,0,0]], a horizontal line of three live cells.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Cell (0,1) sees three live neighbours and is born: store {{2}}. Its low bit remains 0 for later neighbour counts.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The centre (1,1) has two live neighbours and survives: store {{3}}. Both endpoints have only one and retain low-bit-only value 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Cell (2,1) also has three neighbours and becomes {{2}}. After all encodings the board is [[0,2,0], [1,3,1], [0,2,0]].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Shift all entries right: [[0,{{1}},0], [0,{{1}},0], [0,{{1}},0]], the vertical line.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def life_step(board):\n    if not board or not board[0]:\n        return\n    m, n = len(board), len(board[0])\n    for r in range(m):\n        for c in range(n):\n            live = 0\n            for dr in (-1, 0, 1):\n                for dc in (-1, 0, 1):\n                    nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                    if (dr or dc) and 0 &lt;= nr &lt; m and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; n:\n                        live += board[nr][nc] &amp; 1\n            old = board[r][c] &amp; 1\n            if live == 3 or (old and live == 2):\n                board[r][c] |= 2\n    for row in board:\n        for c in range(n):\n            row[c] &gt;&gt;= 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each cell checks at most eight neighbours and is finalised once, so time is O(mn). The counters and indices occupy O(1) additional space. The board itself is reused to store the new generation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Counting \u003ccode>cell == 1\u003c/code> misses a live neighbour already encoded as \u003ccode>3\u003c/code>; always mask the old bit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Exclude the current cell itself when counting neighbours.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check both coordinate bounds before indexing; negative Python indices otherwise wrap.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Finalising a cell during the first pass destroys old-state information needed by later cells.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A solitary live cell dies because it has no live neighbours; an empty board is a no-op.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Change the birth and survival counts while preserving the two-bit synchronous-update scheme.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A toroidal board wraps both coordinates modulo the dimensions instead of treating the edges as boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:73":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a rectangular matrix, replace every cell in any row or column containing an original zero with zero. Perform the change in place using constant extra storage. For \u003ccode>[[1,0,3], [4,5,6]]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[[0,0,0], [4,0,6]]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rows have equal length and are independent mutable lists. Values need only support comparison with zero and assignment of zero. New zeros must not themselves cause additional rows or columns to be cleared: the affected sets are determined from the original matrix. The implementation handles an empty matrix and zero columns as no-ops.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Zero every row and column that contains a zero, in place, with constant extra memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You need a flag per row and per column. Is there a row and a column whose cells could store those flags, once you remember what they themselves held?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place matrix transforms\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use row 0 and column 0 as the flag arrays, plus one boolean for whether column 0 itself contained a zero (row 0's own flag lives in cell (0, 0)). Mark flags in one pass, zero the inner cells from the flags, then zero row 0 and finally column 0, so no flag is overwritten before it is read.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First record all original zero-containing row indices and column indices in two sets, then clear every cell whose row or column is recorded. This takes O(mn) time and O(m+n) extra space. Re-scanning the matrix separately for each cell would reduce storage but repeat work. Directly zeroing rows when a zero is encountered is incorrect because new zeros could propagate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the first cell of each row as its row flag and the first cell of each column as its column flag. The shared corner cannot independently represent both the first row and first column, so remember in a separate boolean whether column zero originally contained a zero. The corner itself serves as the first-row flag.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan rows from top to bottom. Before marking a row, inspect its first cell to preserve whether column zero originally contained a zero. For each zero in columns 1 onward, set the corresponding row and column flags to zero. Once this marking pass ends, every inner cell can be cleared if either of its flags is zero. Finally clear the first row if the corner indicates it, then clear the first column if the saved boolean indicates it. This order keeps all flags intact until their consumers have read them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [[1,2,3], [4,0,6], [0,8,9]]. Column 0 already has an original zero in row 2, so save col_zero = {{True}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The zero at (1,1) marks row 1 and column 1: matrix becomes [[1,{{0}},3], [{{0}},0,6], [0,8,9]].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Using the flags, clear inner positions in rows 1 and 2 and column 1: [[1,0,3], [0,0,{{0}}], [0,{{0,0}}]].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Corner is 1, so do not clear all of row 0. Saved col_zero is true, so clear the first column: [[{{0}},0,3], [0,0,0], [0,0,0]].\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def set_zeroes(matrix):\n    if not matrix or not matrix[0]:\n        return\n    m, n = len(matrix), len(matrix[0])\n    col_zero = False\n    for r in range(m):\n        if matrix[r][0] == 0:\n            col_zero = True\n        for c in range(1, n):\n            if matrix[r][c] == 0:\n                matrix[r][0] = 0\n                matrix[0][c] = 0\n    for r in range(1, m):\n        for c in range(1, n):\n            if matrix[r][0] == 0 or matrix[0][c] == 0:\n                matrix[r][c] = 0\n    if matrix[0][0] == 0:\n        for c in range(n):\n            matrix[0][c] = 0\n    if col_zero:\n        for r in range(m):\n            matrix[r][0] = 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The mark and clear passes each visit at most all \u003ccode>m*n\u003c/code> cells, and the final border passes are O(m+n), so time is O(mn) for nonempty dimensions. Only one boolean and loop indices are added: O(1) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A zero originally at the corner sets both first-row and first-column flags; they must remain independent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Clearing the first row or column before the inner cells destroys markers still needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Inspect column zero before setting the current row's flag, or a zero elsewhere in that row is misclassified as an original column-zero entry.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test one-row and one-column matrices: one of the inner loops is empty, but the border logic still applies.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>If O(m+n) space is acceptable, keep separate row and column sets to avoid modifying the grid during discovery.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace every affected row and column with another sentinel, while retaining a distinct test for the original trigger.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:41":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the least strictly positive integer absent from an integer list. The input may contain negative values, zero, duplicates and values much larger than the list. For example, \u003ccode>[3, 4, -1, 1]\u003c/code> is missing \u003ccode>2\u003c/code>, while \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> is missing \u003ccode>4\u003c/code>. Aim for linear time and constant extra workspace, and allow the list to be reordered.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For length \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, only the numbers \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> need dedicated slots. If all of them occur, the answer is \u003ccode>n+1\u003c/code>; otherwise the first absent one is already in that range. This follows from there being only \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> input positions, and it holds even with duplicates or an empty list. Values outside this range cannot be the first missing positive unless the answer is \u003ccode>n+1\u003c/code>, and they need not be placed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The implementation mutates the list but preserves its multiset of values through swaps. It accepts arbitrary Python integers and returns a positive integer. An empty list returns \u003ccode>1\u003c/code>. A read-only input or a requirement to preserve order would require a different trade-off, such as a set or an explicit copy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest positive integer that is absent, in O(n) time and constant extra space, with negatives, zeros and huge values mixed in.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An array of length n can contain at most n distinct positives. Which values can the answer possibly be, and which inputs can you therefore ignore?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Array as its own index\u003c/p>\u003cp>The answer lies in 1..n + 1. Cyclic sort: swap each value v in 1..n into index v - 1 until that slot already holds v, ignoring everything else. Then the first index i with a[i] != i + 1 gives i + 1; if every slot matches, the answer is n + 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Test candidates \u003ccode>1, 2, ...\u003c/code> in order, scanning the whole list for each until one is absent. The answer cannot exceed \u003ccode>n+1\u003c/code>, so this uses O(n²) time in the worst case and O(1) working space. Alternatively, build a set of all values and query the candidates in expected O(n) time with O(n) extra space.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sorting a copy and looking for gaps gives another reference method, but a general sort costs O(n log n) time and the copy needs O(n) space. Even an in-place sort does not meet the requested linear bound. The bounded candidate range suggests using the list positions as a direct-address table instead.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The home of a value \u003ccode>v\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>1..n\u003c/code> is index \u003ccode>v-1\u003c/code>. At each index \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, if the value there is in range and its home does not already hold it, swap it with the value at its home. Reconsider the newly arrived value at \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, because it may also have an unfilled home. If the current value is out of range or its home already contains the same value, move to the next index.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The key invariant is that a correctly placed value never leaves its home. A swap targets the home of the value being moved and is performed only if that home is not already correct. Thus each swap permanently fixes at least one previously incorrect slot. There are at most \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> such swaps across the whole algorithm, even though a \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> loop is nested inside a \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loop. The other work is constant per index and per swap.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When processing stops, any in-range value that occurs has a correct copy at its home: otherwise some occurrence would have had an eligible swap when its index was processed. Later swaps cannot remove a correct value. Scanning positions left to right therefore finds the least absent value as \u003ccode>i+1\u003c/code> at the first mismatch. If every position matches, return \u003ccode>n+1\u003c/code>. The duplicate guard is essential: swapping two equal copies cannot make progress.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start [3, 4, -1, 1], n = 4. At i = 0, value 3 belongs at index 2; swap to get {{[-1, 4, 3, 1]}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0 now holds -1, which has no home in 1..4. Advance to i = 1; value 4 belongs at index 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Swap indices 1 and 3: [-1, {{1}}, 3, {{4}}]. The new value 1 at i = 1 belongs at index 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Swap indices 1 and 0: [{{1, -1}}, 3, 4]. Index 1 is out of range; indices 2 and 3 are home already.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First mismatch is index 1: expected {{2}}, found -1. Return 2. Each swap permanently fixed a distinct home.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def first_missing_positive(a):\n    n = len(a)\n    for i in range(n):\n        while 1 &lt;= a[i] &lt;= n and a[a[i] - 1] != a[i]:\n            home = a[i] - 1\n            a[i], a[home] = a[home], a[i]\n    for i, value in enumerate(a):\n        if value != i + 1:\n            return i + 1\n    return n + 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The first pass performs at most n successful swaps plus n loop exits; the final scan is also linear. Total time is O(n), including adversarial orderings and duplicates. Only the index, home and value variables are used, so auxiliary space is O(1). The list itself is modified in place.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Check \u003ccode>1 &lt;= a[i] &lt;= n\u003c/code> before indexing its home; negative Python indices silently wrap and large indices raise.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check whether the home already contains the same value, not merely whether its index differs from \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>. Equal duplicates otherwise cause an infinite swap loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute \u003ccode>home\u003c/code> before the tuple assignment. Using \u003ccode>a[a[i]-1]\u003c/code> as an assignment target after assigning \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> can use the changed value when Python evaluates targets.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> returns 1, \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code> returns 2, and an array containing only nonpositive numbers returns 1. All values in place returns \u003ccode>n+1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The final mismatch yields its index plus one, not the misplaced number at that index.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>With values guaranteed in \u003ccode>1..n\u003c/code>, identify every missing value by collecting all mismatched home slots after cyclic placement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Preserve the original order by using a set, accepting O(n) extra memory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If an input copy is allowed but the original is read-only, run the same placement method on that copy and count its storage.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:238":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each position in a list of integers, return the product of all values at other positions. Division is not allowed. For \u003ccode>[2, 3, 4]\u003c/code> the output is \u003ccode>[12, 8, 6]\u003c/code>; for \u003ccode>[2, 0, 4]\u003c/code> it is \u003ccode>[0, 8, 0]\u003c/code>. The result array is not counted as auxiliary workspace.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input may contain negative numbers and zeros, so division by a total product is not a robust strategy even if division were permitted. Products are exact Python integers; in a fixed-width language the numeric contract must instead guarantee no overflow or use wider arithmetic. The empty product is 1, so a singleton produces \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code> and an empty input produces \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each output is the product of all other elements, division is not allowed, and the output array does not count as extra space.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split each answer into what lies to its left and what lies to its right.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place reverse, rotate and compact\u003c/p>\u003cp>Fill the output left to right with prefix products (out[i] = product of a[:i]), then sweep right to left with one running suffix product, multiplying it into out[i] before including a[i]. Zeros need no special case.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space besides the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every index, multiply all entries except the one at that index. This performs O(n²) multiplications and requires only the output plus constant workspace. A prefix array and a suffix array reduce time to O(n), but use O(n) additional storage. The output itself can hold one of those arrays.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Split the product for position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> into the product strictly to its left and the product strictly to its right. First fill \u003ccode>out[i]\u003c/code> with the left product, maintaining a running prefix that is updated only after writing the current position. Then traverse from right to left, multiplying \u003ccode>out[i]\u003c/code> by the running right product before including \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> in that product.\u003c/p>\u003cp>At the start of each forward iteration, \u003ccode>prefix\u003c/code> contains exactly the elements preceding \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>. At the start of each backward iteration, \u003ccode>suffix\u003c/code> contains exactly the elements following \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>. Thus the multiplication combines precisely all positions except \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, even when zeros occur. A single zero contributes to every output except its own; two zeros make every output zero automatically.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [2,0,4]. Start prefix = 1. Write left products before multiplying each current value: output becomes {{[1,2,0]}}.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start suffix = 1 at i = 2: out[2] remains 0, then suffix becomes 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1: multiply out[1] by 4, producing {{8}}; then include input 0, making suffix 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: multiply out[0] by 0. Return {{[0,8,0]}}; no division or zero-specific branch is needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def product_except_self(a):\n    out = [1] * len(a)\n    prefix = 1\n    for i, value in enumerate(a):\n        out[i] = prefix\n        prefix *= value\n    suffix = 1\n    for i in range(len(a) - 1, -1, -1):\n        out[i] *= suffix\n        suffix *= a[i]\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The two passes perform O(n) arithmetic operations and the output occupies O(n) space. Apart from the output, the two running products and indices use O(1) auxiliary space in the usual unit-cost arithmetic model. Python's arbitrary-precision multiplication and integer storage can cost more as products grow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Update the running product after using it, or the current element is included in its own answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not divide by an element: zeros break division and it is disallowed by the contract.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check zero, multiple zeros, negative values, a singleton, and the empty list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the caller counts output as workspace, total allocated space is O(n), not O(1).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Replace multiplication with another associative operation and an identity to obtain all-but-one aggregates where order is preserved.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return only the product modulo a given positive modulus, reducing intermediate magnitude when that is the required result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1980":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> distinct binary strings each of length \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, construct any binary string of length \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> that is not in the input. For \u003ccode>[\"00\", \"11\"]\u003c/code>, both \u003ccode>\"01\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"10\"\u003c/code> are valid answers. The answer need not be the lexicographically smallest missing string.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each entry must contain only \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> and have length exactly \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, so character \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> exists in input string \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>. Distinctness is promised but is not actually needed by the construction. With \u003ccode>n &gt;= 1\u003c/code>, there are more binary strings of length \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> than the n supplied strings. The implementation also returns \u003ccode>\"\"\u003c/code> on an empty list under the natural extension of the contract.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Produce any length-n binary string missing from a list of n distinct length-n strings.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You only have to differ from each listed string somewhere. Can you choose one position per string?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Strings as character arrays\u003c/p>\u003cp>Cantor's diagonal argument: make character i the opposite of strings[i][i]. The result differs from string i at position i, so it equals none of them. Build it in a list and join once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) for the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate the \u003ccode>2**n\u003c/code> candidate binary strings and test each against the input, using a set for membership. This can examine exponentially many candidates and needs storage for the input set and generated strings. Enumerating integer encodings is fine as an independent tiny-input checker, but unnecessary when any absent answer is acceptable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Give each input string its own position at which the answer is guaranteed to differ. For each \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, look at \u003ccode>strings[i][i]\u003c/code> and put its opposite binary digit into position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> of the result. The result differs from input string \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> at position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, so it differs from every input string. This is a direct constructive proof and does not rely on testing membership or searching the candidate space.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Building a list of characters followed by one join keeps construction linear. Random selection might repeatedly hit an existing string and has no deterministic bound on the number of attempts. Diagonal construction always makes exactly n character decisions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input [\"010\", \"111\", \"001\"], n = 3. Reserve position i for disagreeing with string i.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: string 0 has {{0}} at position 0, so append 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1: string 1 has {{1}} at position 1, so append 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2: string 2 has {{1}} at position 2, so append 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Join to get {{100}}. It differs from strings 0, 1, and 2 at positions 0, 1, and 2 respectively.\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def missing_binary_string(strings):\n    chars = []\n    for i, s in enumerate(strings):\n        chars.append(\"1\" if s[i] == \"0\" else \"0\")\n    return \"\".join(chars)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n indexed reads, n appends and one join, so time is O(n). The character list takes O(n) auxiliary space and the returned string takes O(n) output space. This implementation does not claim constant auxiliary space; Python strings are immutable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Flipping a fixed column does not guarantee a distinct result for each input; string \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> needs its own position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The construction needs at least n characters in each string and a binary alphabet; validate inputs separately if those promises are absent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not assume the diagonal answer is the smallest missing one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Distinct inputs are unnecessary for correctness, but they are part of the stated input contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For a larger alphabet, choose any symbol different from each diagonal character.\u003c/li>\u003cli>To require the lexicographically smallest absent string, use a search or trie with appropriate missing-subtree information rather than this arbitrary-answer construction.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
